WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:03.240
I hear from lots of
people every day

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:07.760
who are concerned about how
their diet is affecting their health.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.480
They need answers
based on facts.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:11.440
In other words,

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.400
in the peer-reviewed
medical literature,

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.280
and that is what
I’m here for.

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:19.520
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.840
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.040
You may have heard
about a study

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:27.880
suggesting that an enzyme in bananas
that turns bananas brown

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:32.360
can partially destroy a class of nutrients
found in foods like berries.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:36.080
So, should you stop putting
bananas in your smoothies?

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:38.600
Here are some answers.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:45.560
We all know there are recommended daily intakes&nbsp;
of essential nutrients like vitamins to prevent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:54.600
deficiencies, but recently, the first dietary&nbsp;
recommendation for a bioactive food compound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.600 --> 00:01:03.080
was published. Bioactive food compounds are not&nbsp;
necessary for basic human needs but may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:13.640
health benefits. For example, plant metabolites&nbsp;
known as flavonoids. Flavonoids are a type of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:25.920
polyphenol, of which the most commonly consumed&nbsp;
one is flavan-3-ols, also known as just flavanol.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:32.640
A review of the literature suggests that 400 to&nbsp;
600 milligrams a day of these flavanols would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:37.200
beneficial for cardiovascular protection, such&nbsp;
as the potential for improving blood pressure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:45.200
cholesterol, blood sugars, etc. Note that&nbsp;
this is specifically a food guideline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:53.160
not a supplement guideline, though really, it's&nbsp;
actually more like a beverage guideline since,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.160 --> 00:02:00.400
if you look at the primary sources, it's really&nbsp;
tea, green or black, that is the easiest way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:05.880
reach those levels. Though there are certainly&nbsp;
some in a variety of berries and cocoa. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:14.240
there's an enzyme called polyphenol oxidase&nbsp;
that can break down these wonderful polyphenols&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:21.480
like flavonoids. That is what makes certain&nbsp;
fruits and vegetables brown when you cut them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:29.040
such as bananas and potatoes. Now, why would&nbsp;
a plant do that to itself? Well, apparently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:42.400
it is a defense mechanism, part of the plant's&nbsp;
immune system, and when exposed to oxygen, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:48.600
happens is that polyphenol oxidase can oxidize&nbsp;
polyphenols into these breakdown products, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:58.720
have antimicrobial activity. So when a banana gets&nbsp;
bruised, for example, or you cut open an avocado,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:04.720
the polyphenol oxidase can start oxidizing&nbsp;
polyphenols into these defensive compounds that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:10.800
can glom together, forming a dark compound called&nbsp;
melanin, the same class of compounds that darkens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:18.520
the skin of a banana also darkens our skin too.&nbsp;
Okay, so no problem, we can just not eat bananas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:23.000
that have turned brown, right? Because that's&nbsp;
visual evidence that many of the polyphenols&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:32.120
have been lost. Ah, but what if you mix that&nbsp;
banana, which is filled with that polyphenol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:38.720
oxidase enzyme, in a smoothie with polyphenol-rich&nbsp;
foods like berries or cocoa? And why you end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:45.560
with less nutrition. Well, big chocolate-funded&nbsp;
researchers sought to find out, and so what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:54.400
did: They put about a half cup of cocoa's worth of&nbsp;
cocoa flavanol into a banana-containing smoothie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.400 --> 00:04:02.080
compared to the same amount in a berry smoothie&nbsp;
with no banana, compared to the same amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:12.440
cocoa blended into a banana smoothie. Right,&nbsp;
so the same amount of those cocoa goodies, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:19.720
it's almost as if there was no cocoa in there at&nbsp;
all, presumably because the banana enzymes chopped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:28.080
up all the chocolate goodies. Let's not presume,&nbsp;
though. They blended up that chocy banana smoothie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:35.440
again, which was just banana, almond milk, cocoa&nbsp;
flavanol, and then they just measured the level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:44.720
of flavanols as the smoothie sat for an hour,&nbsp;
and the cocoa flavanols plummeted more than 90%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:55.200
a half-life of about 10 minutes. So every&nbsp;
10 minutes, the levels of polyphenols drop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.200 --> 00:05:02.400
in half. Now, how do we know it was for sure the&nbsp;
polyphenol oxidase enzyme? Because then, if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:13.400
add various polyphenol inhibitors, then they block&nbsp;
the effect. Okay, so no problem if you add bananas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:19.320
to your smoothies, just you have to chug them down&nbsp;
before the enzyme does its job, right? I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:26.680
presumably, the enzyme would be deactivated&nbsp;
when it hit the acid bath of our stomach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:34.040
but you don't know until you put it to the test,&nbsp;
and I love that the researchers did all this. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:40.480
they did is this time they had study participants&nbsp;
alternate sips between a straight banana almond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:47.160
milk smoothie and like chocolate milk, chocolate&nbsp;
flavonoids plus the almond note, right? So there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:53.920
was no premixing in the blender between the&nbsp;
banana enzyme and the flavanol, just mixing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.920 --> 00:06:05.680
in the stomach, such an elegant research move.&nbsp;
Okay, so you get 37% less in your bloodstream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:14.320
showing that the banana enzyme can still do some&nbsp;
polyphenol damage in your stomach. And indeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:24.520
in simulated stomach acid conditions, yes, the&nbsp;
polyphenol oxidase is indeed down but not out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:33.840
Okay, so yeah, if you drink some smoothie with a&nbsp;
banana, it's better not to let it sit around, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:40.040
even if you drink it immediately, you can get less&nbsp;
polyphenols in your system than if you had skipped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:49.200
the banana. But bananas make smoothies so rich&nbsp;
and creamy. I mean, anything we can add to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:57.000
smoothie to counter the banana enzyme's effect?&nbsp;
There's one food that has even more polyphenol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:05.720
oxidase activity than banana, and it's a vegetable&nbsp;
that's not a potato. What vegetable turns brown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:15.520
that's not a white potato? Oh, white mushrooms.&nbsp;
White button mushrooms have more polyphenol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:25.400
oxidase activity than even bananas. Now, for&nbsp;
those of you thinking, don't worry, I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:33.200
think I'm expected to be adding any mushrooms to&nbsp;
my smoothies anytime soon. Now, some people do add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:39.800
avocados to smoothies or make like a chocolate&nbsp;
avocado pudding, and here's the question:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:46.920
What if you eat mushrooms with a meal packed with&nbsp;
polyphenol-rich foods? Right, might it mix in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:54.000
your stomach and decrease the absorption, like the&nbsp;
anthocyanin in red cabbage or berries for dessert?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.000 --> 00:08:01.480
Right, and look, you could also be having some&nbsp;
potatoes with your meal, or eggplant with your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:09.240
meal. Now note, though, however, this is for fresh&nbsp;
produce, right? So, you know, we're not worrying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:20.280
about the eggplant, potatoes, and the enzyme is&nbsp;
utterly destroyed by cooking. And look, remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:26.920
we should be cooking our mushrooms anyway, right,&nbsp;
because of the agitoxin. Most of our mushrooms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:30.960
you don't have to cook oyster mushrooms, but&nbsp;
the white and crimini and portobello mushrooms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:36.840
right? So, problem solved, right? You don't have&nbsp;
to worry about cooked potatoes, cooked eggplant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:45.040
cooked mushrooms. Okay, but who wants to cook&nbsp;
their bananas, though? Right, what else could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:49.840
we put in a smoothie instead? My personal favorite&nbsp;
is mango, which you can buy frozen when it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:55.520
in season. Do we have to worry about that&nbsp;
having a polyphenol-eating enzyme? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.520 --> 00:09:02.360
what happens when you cut open a mango? Does&nbsp;
it go brown? No, it doesn't go brown. Mango&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:13.320
has maybe 500 times less than that banana. Now,&nbsp;
the apple certainly doesn't surprise me, right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:20.040
because what happens with apples, turns brown. But&nbsp;
boy, the smoothie study raises so many interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:26.440
questions, right? So, what, forget smoothies,&nbsp;
should you not add bananas to your oatmeal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:31.400
right? Because the bananas in your stomach would&nbsp;
mix with like the berries or the cocoa that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:38.560
put in your oatmeal. And indeed, that is what&nbsp;
we should expect. So, I no longer add bananas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:45.160
to my cran-chocolate pomegranate breakfast bowl.&nbsp;
That was one of my cooking videos, if you missed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:51.560
it. And since, you know, I don't want to lose any&nbsp;
of those polyphenols, there are so many wonderful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:56.480
ingredients in that. I don't want to lose any,&nbsp;
don't want any of those destroyed, that 37%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.480 --> 00:10:03.560
decrease in your stomach when it all mixes with&nbsp;
that enzyme. Okay, but what if you're not making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:12.760
something chocolatey? This study only tested the&nbsp;
effects of the enzyme on cocoa flavanol, like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:18.640
they didn't check to see if mixing bananas with&nbsp;
berries would affect the berry phytonutrients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:27.760
but the presumption is that it would. And so,&nbsp;
hm, what does that mean for like ready-to-drink&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:31.960
bottled smoothie drinkers that you might find&nbsp;
in the store, right, that may be sitting on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:37.320
the shelf? Well, if it's been pasteurized,&nbsp;
then it should be okay, right? Because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:43.080
heat destroys that enzyme. That's why vegetables&nbsp;
are blanched, you know, before they're frozen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:53.200
to destroy the enzymes. Even just heating bananas&nbsp;
to like 70 degrees C, which is like hot tea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:59.080
70° C for 2 minutes in the presence of vitamin&nbsp;
C and citric acid, which might come from lemon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.080 --> 00:11:06.320
juice, for example, would suppress the enzyme by&nbsp;
80%. I'm not suggesting drinking hot smoothies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:12.280
but rather that those pasteurized smoothie drinks,&nbsp;
depending on the ingredients, may not have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:19.800
same problem as fresh-made smoothies. What about&nbsp;
just adding the vitamin C and citric acid alone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:24.840
like putting lemon juice in your smoothie? It only&nbsp;
cuts the enzyme activity by about 133% without the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:32.240
heating step. However, it does work wonders for&nbsp;
apples. Cut apple, you just add some lemon juice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:37.640
and you can cut that activity nearly in half,&nbsp;
explaining why adding lemon juice to your fruit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.640 --> 00:11:43.360
salad, you know, keeps the apples in your fruit&nbsp;
salad from turning brown. Okay, anything we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:48.920
add to a banana smoothie to inhibit the enzyme&nbsp;
so we can still use bananas but somehow block&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:58.400
the enzyme? Well, they used to put sulfites in&nbsp;
fresh fruits and vegetables to block that enzyme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:06.600
until they were banned in 1986, following cases&nbsp;
of sulfite-induced asthma. They are still used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:13.360
in dried fruit, though, to prevent browning.&nbsp;
What, so, okay, sulfites are kind of off the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:22.960
table. But what about what can we do for natural&nbsp;
anti-browning agents to block this puppy? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:31.680
onion extracts can prevent the browning of&nbsp;
pears, but there has got to be something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:39.280
better for your smoothies. And indeed, pineapple&nbsp;
juice does seem to help keep apples from browning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:45.640
and bananas too, for that matter. But that was&nbsp;
after soaking in pineapple juice for three days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:53.840
so it's not clear if it would work right away.&nbsp;
There was a study comparing lemon juice and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.840 --> 00:13:01.160
white wine to prevent browning in pastry dough.&nbsp;
You've heard of hard cider, hard seltzer, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.160 --> 00:13:09.640
the lemon juice appears to beat out the wine.&nbsp;
So, better a lemon squeeze than drinking a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:16.680
hard smoothie. That's the story on bananas and&nbsp;
smoothies. So should you not eat bananas? No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:19.920
you can eat bananas any time, you just don't want&nbsp;
to eat bananas. You just don't want to have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:27.720
bananas in your stomach at the same time you have&nbsp;
other healthy foods like berries, cocoa, and tea.

